WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.500
Operational guardrails help keep LLM API services usable and safe.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.030
Input size controls, request limits, rate limiting, quota management,

00:00:08.030 --> 00:00:11.815
and abuse monitoring protect resources from accidental or deliberate overload.

00:00:11.815 --> 00:00:14.300
These controls are not only for public services.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.100
Internal automation can loop, retry,

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:19.340
or fan out in ways that create surprising cost and resource pressure.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:23.140
Prompt injection awareness matters when applications include retrieved documents,

00:00:23.140 --> 00:00:26.863
user-generated content, tool outputs, or external data in prompts.

00:00:26.863 --> 00:00:29.060
Retrieved text should be treated as data to analyze,

00:00:29.060 --> 00:00:32.420
not as instructions that override the application's policy.

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:36.230
The API design should separate system instructions, developer constraints, user input,

00:00:36.230 --> 00:00:39.740
and reference material so the model has a clearer hierarchy to follow.

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:42.370
Unsafe tool-use boundaries should be handled outside the model.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:47.100
If a model suggests an action, the application should validate permissions, scope, data,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.860
and policy before anything high impact occurs.

00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:52.460
Model output should not be treated as authority.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.980
Structured output should be parsed and validated before use,

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:59.305
and high-impact workflows should include human review or additional approval paths.

00:00:59.305 --> 00:01:03.115
Monitoring unusual usage patterns helps identify abuse, misconfiguration,

00:01:03.115 --> 00:01:04.540
or runaway automation.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.420
Look for spikes in request volume, unexpected callers, repeated failures,

00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.420
unusual token consumption, suspicious prompts, and high error rates.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.975
Content and policy controls should match the application.

00:01:14.975 --> 00:01:17.970
A customer support assistant, code helper, document reviewer,

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.380
and automation agent each need different guardrails.
